Simply
Lightweight interpreted multi-paradigm language with functions that are treated like variables with Lexical scope.
important stuff

fetch() to make api calls.


weird things

using let makes the variable block scoped
White spaces are recognized as text in strings. For example this will store a newline.

  let email = `
`

We can add a variable to a string by using ${variable}}
We have first class functions meaning that we can store them in a variable

  let helloWorld = function() {
    console.log(&quot;Hello World!&quot;)
  };
  helloWorld(); // prints hello world

You can call a function before declaring it, but ya can’t if you want to store it in a variable.

// 1. Perfectly fine
helloWorld(); 
function helloWorld(){...}

// 2. Absolutely illegal 
helloWorld(); 
let helloWorld = function(){...};

There is something called arrow function for shortening the declaration of a function using statements.

  const hello = somebody =&gt; `Hello ${sombody}`;
  hello(Mark); // Hello Mark!

  const computeRectangle = (a, b) =&gt; {
    if(a &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; b &gt;= 0){
      return a * b;
    }
  };

If you want to return an object you need to put the expression in a parentheses

  const person = (name, surname) =&gt; ({
    name: name,
    surname: surname
  });

using this in a arrow method is complicated
You can destructurize an object, turning its member variable into local variables.

  const sandwich =&gt; ({
    ham: &#039;big&#039;,
    cheese: &#039;orange&#039;,
    topings: [&#039;tomato&#039;,&#039;cucumber&#039;]
  });

  let { ham, cheese } = sandwich;
  console.log(ham); // big

  ham = &#039;small&#039;
  console.log(sandwich.ham); // big
  console.log(ham); // small

There is also the reverse

  const name = &#039;Annapurna&#039;;
  const height = 8091;

  const peak = { name, height };

  console.log(peak); // { name: &#039;Annapurna&#039;, height: 8091 }

